 "class_note": "The confusion classes are built from Azure's IPA phoneme symbols, and only en-US returns them named. The counts below come from the five en-US reads and are the first real phoneme evidence in this file — the en-GB single pass of 09-14 to 09-16 returned every symbol EMPTY, so the spelling-based fallback (classify_word) was carrying the classes alone and undercounting badly: i/ii 15 -> 50, s/z 8 -> 44, schwa 7 -> 32, b/v 9 -> 24. The 09-14 read still contributes spelling guesses only (see locale_note). phonemes_before_2026_09_16 holds the old two-pass tallies from before the regression.",
 "locale_note": "SCORING LOCALE IS en-US SINCE 2026-09-16, at the user's decision. Microsoft's docs make en-US the only locale that returns prosody AND named IPA phonemes. Five of the six reads were re-assessed under it on the SAME audio, the SAME stored Whisper transcript and the SAME span reference — only the locale changed. Prosody moved from 52-59 to 81-86, fluency up about 3 points, accuracy and completeness within a point or two either way, and the SHAPE of the series held (09-12 still the weak read). So the locale changed the levels, not the trend. THE 09-14 READ IS STILL en-GB: at 84 minutes it is the longest, and the Azure F0 quota ran out before it could be re-assessed (CancellationReason.Error 1007, 'Quota exceeded'). Its prosody of 59.4 and pron of 80.6 are en-GB numbers and DO NOT belong on the same line as the rest; its accuracy, fluency and completeness are close enough to read with care. Re-run it after the quota resets: .venv-practice/bin/python scripts/rescore-reads.py --only 2026-09-14",
